<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tughlaq dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tughlaq</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tughlaq dynasty ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> from 1320 to the early fifteenth century. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughlaq">Muhammad bin Tughlaq</a> the sultanate reached its greatest territorial extent, but his ambitious schemes, such as the transfer of the capital and the token currency, caused serious disruption. Firuz Shah Tughlaq's later reign was more stable, but the dynasty was severely weakened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-delhi-1398">sack of Delhi</a> in 1398.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty.jpg" alt="Tughlaq dynasty" width="320" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maps created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan Territorial area: पाटलिपुत्र (talk), per Schwar... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Tughlaqs.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
